  "readmeFile": "README.md",
  "releaseNotes": "0.20.0 — MINOR on the 0.x line: deterministic, resumable coordination cycles\nare now a caller-visible engine command (#2133/#2197).\n\nThe new `cycle` surface inspects, registers, advances, updates, and completes source-bound roadmap and\nworkspace units through one typed ledger. Stable provider-cycle identities bind implementation, critique,\nfeedback, candidate head, merge evidence, and durable update history; stale or wrong-cycle artifacts fail\nclosed. The default journal resolves through Git's per-worktree path, so restart/resume works in the linked\nworktrees used by normal workers.\n\nProvider validation executes only the canonical validators shipped beside the engine, with pinned byte\nidentities. Caller-controlled artifact roots cannot substitute executable validators, tampered bundled\nvalidators are refused, and the SDD provider is bound to the supported 1.0.0 tool/report/work identity.\n\nPREVIOUS RELEASE NOTES (0.19.1):\n\n0.19.1 — PATCH: coherent Blocked parking is now enforced consistently across every board-writing\ntransport route (#2109/#2184).\n\nThe packaged coordination engine rejects an incoherent `Status: Blocked` write before it can leave a\nboard row parked without a concrete blocker or the explicit human/action sentinel. The centralized\ncoverage inventory includes add, release, reconcile, and restored-status writers, so future transport\nroutes cannot silently bypass the same guard. This is a compatibility-preserving correctness patch to\n0.19.0's caller-visible lifecycle contract.\n\nPREVIOUS RELEASE NOTES (0.18.0):\n\nRuntime changes merged after 0.17.0 make the packaged engine match the coordination and product-board\nguidance already carried by Kit 0.24.0 and the driver manifest.\n\nREPOSITORY-SCOPED TOUCH SETS (#2011): path declarations are interpreted in their declared Repo Scope,\nso identical relative paths in different repositories no longer contend accidentally.\n\nBOARD FACTS AND DIAGNOSTICS (#2040/#2042): scheduler facts are extracted behind a typed application\nboundary, overlap refusals name the reserving holder, and board drivers receive structured rate-limit\nclasses instead of parsing prose (#2041).\n\nSAFE RECONCILIATION (#1984/#2000/#2037): blocker-cleared repairs fail closed when the live PR probe\ncannot be read, lint covers blocked verdicts, and deliberate human parks are distinguished from stale\nblockers.\n\nCOMMAND AND SCHEDULER CORRECTIONS (#1991/#1992/#1993/#2031): option ownership and rendered flag\nspellings agree with the command contract, dead scan rendering is removed, and filing a single-item\nparallel lane is diagnosed rather than silently presented as fan-out.\n\nPatch is not defensible: the 0.x tool gains result vocabulary, repository-scoped scheduling semantics,\nand caller-visible refusal/diagnostic behavior.\n\nPREVIOUS RELEASE NOTES (0.17.0):\n\nTWO merged engine commits since 0.16.0.\n\nREFUSAL SPELLINGS (#1573): refusal text is now derived from the command-contract flag spelling table,\nincluding the non-long `-n` limit spelling. Contract output and a refusal can no longer silently name\ndifferent flags.\n\nSAFE ROTATED-SESSION RECOVERY (#1857): a live claim carrying this worker id but a different session\nstill refuses — the #419 twin guard is not weakened — but its diagnostic now names the marker session\nand the `CLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_ID`/`FSGG_WORKER` pin needed for a worker whose harness rotated sessions.\nIt explicitly warns not to mint a new id and strand the live claim.\n\nPatch is not defensible: both changes alter caller-visible diagnostic/recovery behavior on a 0.x tool.\nNo Protocol.fs wire-surface change is included.\n\nPREVIOUS RELEASE NOTES (0.16.0):\n\nEIGHT merged engine commits since 0.15.0 across 32 source/project files. The #1900 squash commit\nconsolidates four reviewed input commits, making eleven pre-merge inputs in total. Protocol.fs moved,\nso the generated worker contract and the published engine were out of step until this release.\n\nFOLLOW-UP QUEUES AND THE DONE BOUNDARY (.github#1900/#1952): `followup audit` now observes every local\nqueue, previews reconciliation, and applies safe removal only after a durable GitHub disposition is\nverified. `done` audits residual promises before stamping completion. `--apply` is now valid for the\nfollowup command, and the JSON/text result vocabulary gains the audit and reconciliation outcomes.\n\nFAIL-CLOSED MARKER SCANS (.github#1896/#1949): scheduler selection, claim/adopt/reap checks, and active\ntouch-set decisions no longer continue from a partial comment scan. A previously successful operation\nmay now refuse with an incomplete/undetermined result rather than silently ignoring a hidden claim.\n\nSCHEDULING AND RANKING: BLOCKER-CLEARED respects an in-flight implementation (.github#1738/#1921);\ndecision items stay out of the worker queue (.github#1887/#1946); Severity is an ordered rank axis\n(.github#1901); and `lint` names consolidation candidates when overlapping work is one cause\n(.github#1915).\n\nSKILL AND KIT DIAGNOSTICS: skill views are derived from receiver declarations (.github#1948), and KIT\nDIGEST gives correct claim advice for skill files (.github#1942).\n\nKNOWN RESIDUE: .github#1892 still decides whether secondary rate limits receive a distinct exit code or\nremain EX_RATE with richer data. .github#1857 still tracks a session id rotating mid-item and stranding\nthe worker's own heartbeat/release/done operations. Neither behavior is claimed fixed by this release.",
